P?S/8 supports binary files in a variety of contexts, primarily as input to the multi-purpose binary loader program known as P?S/8 \i BIN\i0 .\par
\par
The binary file format is known as \i slurp\i0  binary; this term was coined by its inventor, the legendary Richard Lary, generally known as the best PDP-8 programmer in the history of mankind.  The term is an attempt to explain how the loader works without requiring a buffer, even when loading from certain storage devices traditionally associated with block-oriented access.  In\par
P?S/8, each system device generally has a companion slurp loader which is used by the BIN utility to load binary files on any particular system.  This is a customized routine geared to the same device as the system device handler for the express purpose of loading binary files.\par
\par
Note: The P?S/8 BIN \strike progtam \cf2\strike0 program \cf3 ok\cf2  \cf1 should not be confused with the name of standard binary paper-tapes which are generally loaded by the BIN loader.  That said, P/S/8 BIN does support certain operating modes that handle BIN (and RIM) binary paper-tapes, including the conversion of BIN format paper-tapes from/to P?S/8 slurp-format binary files.  When the terms \i BIN\i0  and \i file\i0  are used in the same sentence, this refers to slurp format binary files.\line\line The advantages of the custom loader include:\line\line 1) No reserved memory areas beyond the prevailing system convention (which is\par
