\cf3 The notion is that unlike many other computers, generally PDP-8 systems are nearly completely non-resident, so the system and the programs both use most of the memory, just not at the same time, and of course there are no hardware "protection" schemes so successful operation depends on total cooperation and no blunders.  The standards are well defined.  The system kernel owns 07600-07777 and programs of all forms can do what they want in the rest.  That also means a program can have interrupts on as it has to load its own interrupt handler at 0 and 1 and pointing to the interrupt service routines at still-higher addresses of the program's choosings, such as FOCAL or real-time experiments or whatever.\par
\line The system kernal can provide various services for the benefit of programs that care about them and where relevant, total program conformance depends on observing the relevant parameter values. but a very loose interpretation generally gets a working program that is not flexible.  This is just a pre-cursor to what it takes for example to get a PDP-8 program to run on a DECmate without being broken.  Only P?S/8 supports this and why there are so many different versions of OS/8,78, 278, etc, all of which are broken.  OS/8 is the "wild west" where even the console device has no standards, which is why things are sloppy and don't always work even on fully compatible systems and forget Control-S/Control-Q protocol while P?S/8 is FANATICALLY lock-step compatible.  Which would you rather have! [Rhetorical]\line\line Thus, part of what is in the kernel is that starting at 07600 requires only field 0 data field, AC and L ignored, and in general interrupts need to be off, but whatever is there gets the entire system back up, and in the case of P?S/8 gets you the keyboard monitor and the ability to type commands, unless BATCH is still in effect in which case it is still running off the contents of passed command input files until the last one runs out.  Actually, to be picky, it matters if BATCH has the /E option set, because without the switch, the reboot ends the batch, while the other way, it actually saves the entire BATCH context on reserved blocks and restore the batch as well!\line\line In any case, all of the above is what the system wants to do with what it reloads, and of course execution commands make all of that go away and be replaced by the system program requested, but the system kernel never leaves.\line\line Now to include necessary confusion, there is an exception, when the non-virtual slurp loader is in the kernel area, it loads the binary files into memory and when done there is no useful kernel, other than you can still start at 07600 and it will reload everything, but this is a more "stand-alone" rebooter because the kernel routines are not there to help out in their own reload process, so in general these are DIFFERENT code words to get the same end results.\line\line If the /I option is in effect, the file list is forced to end with a "special file" that is actually the contents of block 0060 which is a one-block long slurp file that automagically replaces the slurp loader with the normal system device handler contents of the kernel area.  Sounds like counter-intuitive magic, but it does work.  Richie invented Slurp, I invented the reloader.  That's why the normal 17 max files are limited to 16 max because the last file is block 60 not actually in the directory and also not % or $.  [It is block 60, the other two are 20 and 40 respectfully.]\line\line But after /I code in block 60 gets the handler back into the kernel area, then it is instantly available again, but at that point, the file info used to get there is irrelevant and in general no longer present [you have no right to care what got your program in, suffice that it IS in!\line\line The primary purpose of loading a program is to do some specific defined purpose, this is NOT a system program, whose primary purpose is to process input or output or both files for a more P?S/8-specific purpose such as PAL assembly.  That is why the system device handler has to always be there in the kernel before and and after.\line\line Nearly all of what a system program is can be temporarily contrived using the R $ or R % command interchangeable passing files and switches and =parameter.  You use GET files and that saves an image of field 0 in % and $ and gets loaded by this special command[s].  The only things that CANNOT be done are:\line\line 1) overlays.  It has to be self-contained in as much as all of field 0, all of which will always be loaded.\line\line 2) There are two special execution attributes that cannot be supported.\line\line a) You cannot force the =xxxx command to be in decimal, technically it is either octal or in the current radix, which COULD be decimal, and generally is, but in this restrictive mode it MUST be octal.\line\line b) There are options regarding parsing restrictions to no files, parse the command line yourself, such as the SET command does, or only output files are parsed as in the BSAVE command.  In this restrictive mode, neither is available.\line\line 3) The starting address is IMPLICITLY 00200 and cannot be specified.  All else is as good as a full system program gets.  This is why this is a useful thing.\line\line I intend to remove certain system programs that really can conform to this and instead relegate them to slurp binary file collections to save space on crowed system devices such as DECtapes.  At the minimum this means DECtape formatters and the DTCOPY program that can copy 384 word blocks from 16/18/36 bit systems.  And also such as RK8E formatters.  Then, if it is complicated, include a BATCH command file that will setup the requirements and documents what to do with the R $ or % command to get it going.  It may require as many as 3 files and a batch command file, but put thi on a primarily seldom-used files logical unit to make the usually used system bootable device tape as lean and mean as possible.  This is especially true given the SHELL direction going in the next release, etc.  But if the system device is huge, such as the RK8E system, all of this is pretty much moot as there is more room than required!  But this is the nature of a system with many devices and bigger devices.  And the RX01 is even smaller!\par
