 \cf1 Figure 1 shows the contents of block 0020 just after the assembly of the example file.  A description of each word is as follows:\line\line 00) 5340 - This is the first control word in the block which indicates the\par
    disposition of the next six data words.\par
\par
01) 0200 - All binary files automatically generate an opening *200 origin\par
    setting.  This is universally the case with all PDP-8 assemblers.\par
\par
02) 0200 - P?S/8 PAL outputs the prevailing current origin at the start of\par
    each binary output file.  In this example, this is redundant to the\par
    previous word only because this is the first (and only) output file.  The\par
    prevailing field setting is contained within the slurp loader; it is\par
    initialized to load into field zero by default.\par
\par
Note: Future releases of P?S/8 PAL may also include the prevailing field setting after the prevailing origin setting.  Future releases of P?S/8 BIN will include corresponding changes when converting binary paper-tapes to P/S/8 slurp format binary files.\par
\par
03) 6271 - The source program starts with a FIELD 7 directive; as such, a\par
    corresponding CDF 70 (6271) instruction is placed in the output file. \par
\par
Note: Most PDP-8 assemblers can place an automatic *200 after FIELD directives; this is generally not necessary as good programming practice demands the use of a new origin setting.  OS/8 PAL8 and P?S/8 PAL include option switches to enable this feature; by default this does not happen to minimize the binary file size.  Files intended to be punched to binary paper-tapes and ultimately loaded by the DEC "self-starting" binary loader must enable this feature to avoid a fatal loading bug which was never fixed.\cf2   Now folks will want the details of the never-fixed fatal loading bug!  I do.  ;)\page\cf1\page\cf3 In a nutshell, The FIELD setting has to be in effect AFTER it happens.  The nature of the general flow of the BIN program is to double-buffer to not accidentally load the checksum, but they turn that into a "feature" so it self-starts if the very last frame pair is an origin setting, this the name self-starting.\line\line But there is a serious bug, the new field CDF instruction applies too soon thus, the last data word is instead loaded into the NEW FIELD which is totally wrong!  By protecting against the bug by inserting an extraneous *200 after the field setting, it avoids the bug because origins store directly into the pointer, not indirectly, so the CDF instruction doesn't apply, even if wrong.  DCA PTR doesn't use the DF, only DCA I PTR, so the origin setting works despite the wrong data field which for that one operation is not used.  In any case, it's an option, and years ago, many assemblers ALWAYS did the origin after a field settting so they got away with it for quite awhile.  P?S/8 PAL and PAL8 both have options to turn it on and off.  Read the PAL manual you are working on for more info on that!\par
\par
\par
