\par
06) 6203 - The source program includes a CIF CDF 00 instruction.\par
\par
07) 0125 - This is the second (and final) control word in the block (and\par
    entire program) which indicates the disposition of the next six data\par
    words.  (Actually, this is partially moot as the end-of-file is indicated\par
    within the control word.)\par
\par
10) 5603 - The program includes a JMP I 0203 instruction.\line\line 11) 7600 - The program includes the address value of 7600 used to exit the\par
    program.\par
\par
12) xxxx - The end-of-file point has been reached.  Words 012-175 are ignored.\par
    Additionally, words 176-177 are ignored in all slurp binary file blocks.\cf2   I think my "study" (a separate text file) is correct because it seems to agree with all of this word-by-word explanation.\line\line\cf3 Yes, only the opening 7 word groups are data, and even then only until an EOF condition, although by practice, the control word sets all the rest of the bits to 01 even though only the first one matters [was easy to do in the code, so why not].\line\line So, junk after eof, and two words after groups of 7 are ignored, and in the case of DECtape also the 129th word is ignored, but all P?S/8 code avoids the 129th word because you are NOT obligated to transfer it and generally do not.  But the slurp loader can stop any operation when the data is logically done, and move on to the next file's first block, etc. until they are all processed and then it just stops the tape and no more tape motion [but /I has its own agenda to reload the system handler!].\par
